# Jira tickets — every ticket carries a QA checklist

Every PROD ticket's description has a **QA checklist** section, placed directly
after its Acceptance criteria. It tells a team member who did not build the work
how to verify it. Then the developer can hand the ticket to a technical BA or a
non-technical BA without walking them through it.

Added 2026-10-07. That day the checklist was backfilled on PROD-2751 and its
sub-tasks, the PROD-2752 Epic and its children, and PROD-2900. Before that, test
plans lived in comments (PROD-2753 comment 27620) or nowhere.

## When it applies

| Moment | What to do |
|---|---|
| `createJiraIssue` (any type: Story, Task, Bug, Sub-task, Epic) | Write the checklist with the AC. Set **Ready to verify: No — not started**. Write the steps from the AC only. Do not invent implementation details. |
| Work delivered (PR merged or opened, data loaded, script run) | Update the checklist to match what was **actually built**: real URLs, PR numbers, commands and IDs. |
| Before a gated forward transition (`jira-transition-gate.md`) | The checklist must be current. List it in the confirmation block. A stale or missing checklist means the ticket is not ready. |
| A "BA test plan" was posted as a comment | Move it into the description's checklist. Comments are not where people look for test plans. |

## Format (headings exact; ADF heading 2 for the title, heading 3 below it)

```
## QA checklist
**Ready to verify:** Yes — <where> | No — <what must happen first>
**Where:** <env + URL, e.g. https://staging.pakfactory.com (sign in to Vercel first); Studio/admin URL; dataset>
**Access needed:** <accounts / roles>

### Non-technical BA
| # | Steps | Expected result | Pass/Fail |
  - Use concrete clicks and pages, and describe what "good" looks like on screen.
  - No terminal, no code, no DB access.
  - Map each row to an AC ("AC 2").
  - For a bug: reproduce the original report, confirm the fix, then run a regression check on 2+ other records.

### Technical BA
  - Commands, DevTools/Lighthouse checks, SQL/GROQ, API calls, logs, PRs to review.
  - Give the expected output for each.
  - Read-only by default. If a step writes, name the environment; never prod.
  - Anything verified only with mocks or locally gets a real-environment check here.

### Not in scope / known gaps
  What not to fail the ticket for, and which ticket covers it. List deferred or unmet AC.

### Unmet acceptance criteria — reporter decision   (only when delivered work misses an AC)
  Overall achieved rating: N/5 — 5 fully met + verified on staging · 4 met, verified
  only locally · 3 most value delivered, target missed · 2 partial · 1 started, little value.
  Per unmet AC: what WAS achieved (measured) · why it was not met (real cause) ·
  options with effort S/M/L and gain (extra work here / follow-up ticket / accept +
  amend AC) · recommendation and whether it is worth it.
  End: "Decision: reporter to choose" — left unanswered. Post one comment that
  @mentions the reporter only, pointing to it. Never decide it, never create the
  follow-up ticket unasked.

### Evidence to attach
  What the verifier posts back as a comment: screenshots, Lighthouse JSON, query output.
  Report failures as a comment with the page, test #, browser/device and a screenshot.
```

## Rules

1. **Never invent identifiers.** If a PR number, URL, deploy ID or record ID is
   not in the ticket, its comments or git/gh, write `TBD — developer to fill in`.
   See the memory `feedback-never-infer-an-identifier`.
2. **Edit in ADF, insert only.** Fetch the description as ADF, insert the section
   after the AC, write the full ADF back, then re-read the ticket to confirm the
   original text survived. A markdown round-trip can flatten tables and panels.
   The Rovo `getJiraIssue` may return markdown even when asked for ADF (seen
   2026-10-07 on PROD-2900). In that case, rebuild the ADF carefully. Before
   writing, check the `attachment` field and any inline images, panels or
   mentions. Compare the markdown before and after the edit.
3. **One checklist per ticket.** If one already exists, update it in place.
4. **Parents and Epics** get an index: one row per child with key, status and
   ready-to-verify. Add an end-to-end check where the parent has its own outcome.
5. **Updating the checklist is not a transition.** Editing the description is
   ungated. Moving the ticket is still gated by `jira-transition-gate.md`.

## Skills that carry it

- `jira-create-ticket` (`~/.claude/skills/`): owns ticket creation, including the checklist template.
- `finisher` (`internalPortal-dataEngine/.claude/skills/` + `.cursor/skills/`): step 4c-1b updates the checklist on delivery; the 4d create template has AC + QA checklist sections.
- `wayfinder` (`pakfactory.com-poc`, `poc-aslan`: `TRACKER-JIRA.md`) also calls `createJiraIssue`, for planning-question tickets. It is deliberately not wired in, because AC and a QA checklist do not fit them (Richard, 2026-10-07).

